{"text": " You wake up in 1948 on a hospital ward in Britain. Patients with tuberculosis lie in rows. The only sound is coughing. Did you know that here the doctors don't choose who gets the new drug? A table of random numbers does. Here's your patient. A new drug, Streptomycin, is scarce. There isn't enough for everyone. So a statistician, Austin Bradford Hill, makes a plan. The choices are made by random numbers, sealed in envelopes. The doctors admitting patients can't see them. One group gets the drug and bed rest. The other gets bed rest alone. Then they count. How long do you live in Britain? A newborn could expect about 68 years. So what is medicine now, proof? Don't believe it. Test it fairly. Six months later, the numbers are in. You've already guessed them. Were you right? First, one more morning, one where a disease disappears.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 3.32, "text": " You wake up in 1948 on a hospital ward in Britain.", "tokens": [50364, 509, 6634, 493, 294, 38833, 322, 257, 4530, 15234, 294, 12960, 13, 50562], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11156201550341029, "compression_ratio": 1.5231316725978647, "no_speech_prob": 0.02052789367735386, "words": [{"word": " You", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.14, "probability": 0.7528867125511169}, {"word": " wake", "start": 0.14, "end": 0.3, "probability": 0.9805624485015869}, {"word": " up", "start": 0.3, "end": 0.5, "probability": 0.9993388056755066}, {"word": " in", "start": 0.5, "end": 0.6, "probability": 0.9971074461936951}, {"word": " 1948", "start": 0.6, "end": 1.44, "probability": 0.9880289435386658}, {"word": " on", "start": 1.44, "end": 2.12, "probability": 0.7885857224464417}, {"word": " a", "start": 2.12, "end": 2.24, "probability": 0.9929401874542236}, {"word": " hospital", "start": 2.24, "end": 2.62, "probability": 0.9371074438095093}, {"word": " ward", "start": 2.62, "end": 2.88, "probability": 0.9638610482215881}, {"word": " in", "start": 2.88, "end": 3.02, "probability":